From 1b7154d9b11c87ab18516321d03f72746b801018 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew DeRosier <andy@skychase.zone>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 22:43:17 +0900
Subject: m68k: Add -mfastcall and the fastcall/cdecl function attributes

Based on the fastcall implementation from the m68k-atari-mint fork.

Under fastcall the first three integer arguments are passed in d0-d2 and
the first two pointer arguments in a0-a1 (fp0-fp2 for the first three
floating-point arguments when an FPU is present); the remaining arguments
go on the stack as in the standard ABI.  Return values are unchanged.

d2 becomes caller-saved: the caller has to overwrite it to pass the third
argument, so a callee preserving it would preserve a value the caller
already destroyed.  This is a convention change, and every object in a
program must agree on it.

Libcalls, including every call into libgcc, keep the standard ABI, since
libgcc is compiled without fastcall.  Sibcalls are allowed only to direct
same-ABI callees: an indirect fastcall sibcall would load its target into
a0, which is now an argument register.  Nested functions under fastcall
are rejected with `sorry'.

